---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="Recording sessions"
   summary="Record a real VoiceOver session to MOV or an NVDA session to MP4. Pair the video with the session transcript in a bug or test report."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section>
      <h2>Supported targets</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>VoiceOver on macOS with a <code>.mov</code> output path</li>
         <li>NVDA on Windows with an <code>.mp4</code> output path</li>
         <li>The <code>sr start --recording</code> command</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
         The virtual screen reader target does not support recording. It runs headlessly and
         produces transcript data instead.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Examples</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --target voiceover --recording ./recordings/voiceover-home.mov --json
a1 sr read --session &lt;sessionId&gt; --json
a1 sr stop --session &lt;sessionId&gt; --json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Setup with doctor</h2>
      <p>
         On macOS, <code>doctor</code> runs a short native recording probe for the current host app.
         It also reports how a11ied will handle browser-backed automation. The browser policy checks
         for an existing local Chromium-family browser before Playwright Chromium.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor
a1 doctor --json
npx playwright install chromium</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
      <p>
         If recording fails, run <code>doctor</code> first. On macOS, a11ied records video without
         microphone input. If recording works from Terminal but fails from your editor or agent,
         check that the host app has Screen Recording permission in System Settings. If a browser-backed
         command fails before it gets to recording, install Chromium with
         <code>npx playwright install chromium</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Privacy notes</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>Recordings may capture page content, system chrome, and assistive technology output.</li>
         <li>A recording documents one specific test run. Include the artifact path in the test report.</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <h2>What a recording captures</h2>
      <p>
         A recording shows the page, browser, and screen reader activity from one test run. Use the
         transcript to review the spoken output.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
